  "id": "DrugBank_DB00287 - MONDO_5338",
  "verbalization": "Travoprost, a prostaglandin analog, is implicated in the treatment of open-angle glaucoma through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Travoprost is associated with ocular hypertension, a condition that is a precursor to open-angle glaucoma. This relationship is supported by the indication links to latanoprostene bunod, befunolol, and dorzolamide, all of which are known to influence intraocular pressure. The shared mechanism of action in these drugs—modulating aqueous humor production or outflow—suggests a potential role in the pathophysiology of open-angle glaucoma. The high scores from the three paths indicate a consistent and robust association, though the exact mechanism by which Travoprost contributes to the development of open-angle glaucoma remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of targeting ocular hypertension as a potential therapeutic strategy for glaucoma.",
